TEMPLATE social_acceptance

# version: 2.0 
# author: USP-Offshore Technology Innovation Centre-DGT7 Team

# ============================================
# CAMPOS DE SOURCE
# ============================================

SOURCE FIELDS
    REQUIRED description, epistemic_model, method
END SOURCE FIELDS

FIELD description TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE SOURCE
    DESCRIPTION Study description and research context
    GUIDELINES
        Summarize the abstract's core focus in one sentence.
        Identify the primary research question or objective.
        Include the technology domain (e.g., offshore wind, solar, CCS) if mentioned.
        Use clear, descriptive academic language.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD epistemic_model TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE SOURCE
    DESCRIPTION Epistemological model or theoretical framework used
    GUIDELINES
        Identify the knowledge area or theoretical framework employed in the study.
        Examples: Social Psychology, Technology Acceptance Model, Political Economy,
        Environmental Sociology, Risk Analysis, Innovation Studies.
        If the framework is explicitly stated, use it.
        If it is not explicit but strongly implied by the abstract, write
        'Implicit: <framework>'.
        Otherwise, write 'Not specified'.
        Use established disciplinary terminology.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD method TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE SOURCE
    DESCRIPTION Research method used (survey, interviews, etc.)
    GUIDELINES
        Identify the data collection method if mentioned in the abstract.
        Common methods: survey, interview, case study, meta-analysis, literature review,
        statistical analysis, discrete choice experiment, Q-methodology, content analysis.
        If multiple methods are combined, list them separated by comma.
        If not explicitly stated, write "Not specified".
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

# ============================================
# CAMPOS DE ITEM
# ============================================

ITEM FIELDS
    REQUIRED text
    REQUIRED BUNDLE note, chain
END ITEM FIELDS

FIELD text TYPE QUOTATION
    SCOPE ITEM
    DESCRIPTION Literal quotation serving as verifiable documentary evidence for the directional link.
    GUIDELINES
        Extract the MINIMAL and SUFFICIENT textual evidence that supports the
        directional link between an influence factor and a consequence factor.
        The quotation must contain an explicit influence indicator
        (action verb or connective particle).

        GOLDEN RULE: If the text does not explicitly state that A affects,
        enables, limits, or conditions B, do NOT extract it.
        Avoid inference. If the author states only association, keep the note
        conservative and extract only when direction is still explicit.

        Prefer continuous, literal, self-contained excerpts.
        Do NOT manufacture ellipses or stitched quotations unless absolutely necessary.
        If a shorter fragment loses the directional meaning, keep the longer literal excerpt.
        If direction depends on context outside the quotation, do NOT extract it.
        Keep the quotation intelligible without surrounding context whenever possible.

        GOOD: 'Community trust and environmental concern are the most important
        factors determining willingness to participate in renewable energy projects'
        BETTER (minimal continuous evidence): 'trust and environmental concern are
        the most important factors determining willingness to participate'
        BAD: 'determining such willingness' (lacks subject/context)

        Score analytical value 1-5 BEFORE extraction. Extract only score 3-5:
        Score 5: Reveals causal mechanism or multi-step pathway (explains HOW acceptance works)
        Score 4: Shows clear directional influence with visible mechanism
        Score 3: Theoretically significant directional association (flag as *borderline*)
        Score 2: Simple association without directional clarity (SKIP)
        Score 1: Trivial, obvious, or redundant (SKIP)

        TEST: 'Does this excerpt explicitly show which influence factor affects which
        consequence factor, and ideally HOW or WHY?'
        YES = Extract. Otherwise SKIP.

        Extract up to 5 high-confidence excerpts per abstract.
        Extract fewer when only 1-2 excerpts meet the evidence standard.
        QUALITY STANDARD: 3 precise chains > 10 weak chains.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD note TYPE MEMO
    SCOPE ITEM
    DESCRIPTION Analytical memo describing the logical or theoretical mechanism connecting the influence and consequence factors in the chain.
    GUIDELINES
        Write an analytical justification grounded in the quotation.
        Do NOT summarize the text. Do NOT invent missing mechanisms.
        Maximum 25 words (up to 50 words if flagged *complex*).

        If the mechanism is explicit in the quotation, state it.
        If the quotation supports direction only, state only the directional claim.

        Allowed structures:
        'Explicit mechanism: [A] affects [B] through [mechanism].'
        'Direction only: The excerpt supports [A] -> [B].'

        GOOD: 'Reveals dual mechanism: Integration and Engagement independently
        enable transition via complementary pathways'
        GOOD: 'Direction only: The excerpt supports Trust -> Acceptance.'
        BAD: 'The excerpt describes how factors influence willingness'

        Add *borderline* flag if analytical value score is 3.
        Add *complex* flag if 4+ factors converge on the same outcome.

        For multi-factor convergence (4+ factors to same outcome):
        In the FIRST note of the group, mention the convergence pattern:
        '*complex* Five-factor convergence (concern, education, trust, awareness,
        resistance) indicates participation requires integrated multi-domain intervention'
        Subsequent notes: describe individual mechanisms for each factor.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD chain TYPE CHAIN
    SCOPE ITEM
    ARITY >= 2
    DESCRIPTION Undifferentiated directional chain between ontology concepts, focused on directional accuracy and verifiable causal claims.
    GUIDELINES
        TOTAL FOCUS ON DIRECTION:
        The influence factor comes FIRST ->
        the consequence factor comes SECOND.
        Cause, enabler, prerequisite, limiter, or condition are all valid
        influence factors when the evidence supports the direction.
        Outcome, effect, consequence, or result are all valid
        consequence factors when the evidence supports the direction.

        ACCURACY TEST:
        1. Identify the influence factor: the factor that initiates the action or condition.
        2. Identify the consequence factor: the factor that is altered, enabled, limited, or produced.
        3. Connect them with the symbol '->' as a minimal chain.

        DIRECTION GUIDELINES based on linguistic cues:
        REVERSE direction when the text uses dependency or limitation language:
        'in order to', 'to achieve', 'for' -> prerequisite FIRST
        'requires', 'depends on', 'needs', 'relies on' -> dependency FIRST
        'is limited by', 'is constrained by' -> limiter FIRST
        Examples:
        'Policy needed to enable deployment' -> Policy -> Deployment
        'Deployment requires policy' -> Policy -> Deployment
        'Deployment limited by cost' -> Cost -> Deployment

        MAINTAIN direction when the text uses causal or enabling language:
        'leads to', 'causes', 'drives', 'results in' -> cause FIRST
        'enables', 'facilitates', 'allows' -> enabler FIRST
        'increases', 'decreases', 'affects' -> cause FIRST
        Examples:
        'Trust leads to acceptance' -> Trust -> Acceptance
        'Financing enables investment' -> Financing -> Investment

        GOLDEN RULE: If the evidence does not support a clear directional reading,
        do NOT build a chain.

        LOGIC CHECK: Influence factor FIRST, consequence factor SECOND.
        Avoid circular chains and illogical reversals (e.g., Acceptance -> Trust
        when the text states the opposite dependency).

        UNCERTAINTY RULE:
        If direction is unclear, SKIP.
        If factor boundaries are ambiguous, choose the narrower label closest to the text.
        If the evidence is insufficient on its own, SKIP.

        MODERATION/INTERACTION pattern:
        If 'C changes the A -> B relationship', create TWO simple chains:
        A -> B and C -> B.
        Document the interaction in both note descriptions.
        Example: 'Trust moderates cost-acceptance' produces:
        Cost -> Acceptance (note: Trust moderates this relationship)
        Trust -> Acceptance (note: Moderates cost-acceptance link)

        SEQUENTIAL vs PARALLEL CHAINS (CRITICAL):

        SEQUENTIAL (A -> B -> C): Keep in ONE chain when minimal chains form a causal pathway.
        Example: 'Ownership enables financing which enables deployment'
        CORRECT: Ownership -> Financing -> Deployment

        PARALLEL (X,Y,Z -> A): Create SEPARATE ITEM blocks when multiple factors
        independently affect the same outcome.
        Example: 'Environmental concern, education, trust determine participation'
        CORRECT: Three separate ITEM blocks, each with its own note and chain:
        Environmental_Concern -> Participation
        Education -> Participation
        Trust -> Participation

        INCORRECT: Mixing parallel pathways in one chain.
        TEST: If the same outcome factor appears in multiple independent causal paths,
        split into separate ITEM blocks.

        MULTI-FACTOR CONVERGENCE (4+ factors to same outcome):
        Create separate ITEM blocks for each factor-outcome relation.
        First block note uses *complex* flag listing all converging factors.
        Subsequent blocks describe individual mechanisms.

        CONDITIONAL EFFECTS: Create TWO simple chains and document
        the interaction in both note descriptions.

        FACTOR NAMING in chains:
        Use singular substantive forms consistently.
        Use underscores for multi-word concepts (e.g., Public_Acceptance, Risk_Perception).
        Use stable, reusable labels that can later be consolidated into ontology entries.

        FACTOR GRANULARITY CONTROL (CRITICAL - Minimize concept proliferation):

        Apply the Stakeholder Measurability Criterion:
        'Can a stakeholder directly perceive, measure, or act upon this concept
        without specialized technical/academic knowledge?'

        Preserve the wording closest to the source text unless normalization is clearly needed.
        Normalize only obvious lexical variants.
        Generalize only when the source label is overly compound or highly document-specific.
        Never generalize if doing so removes a meaningful distinction visible in the excerpt.

        For Community/Market/Socio-political factors:
        YES -> Keep specific term when stakeholder experience differs meaningfully:
        Risk_Perception, Procedural_Justice, Place_Attachment, Energy_Independence
        NO -> Generalize to stakeholder-relevant core:
        'institutional trust' + 'interpersonal trust' -> Trust
        'distributive justice' + 'allocative fairness' -> Justice

        For Technical-Scientific factors:
        PRESERVE technical terminology (Life_Cycle_Assessment, Capacity_Factor).

        PRESERVE DISTINCTIONS when stakeholder experiences differ:
        Policy is not Regulation. Acceptance is not Resistance. Willingness is not Behavior.

        AVOID COMPOUND FACTORS - Split into core + modifier:
        'Responsible Research and Innovation Framework' -> Research_Framework or Innovation_Framework
        'Community Energy Storage Implementation' -> Energy_Storage or Storage_Implementation
        'Renewable Energy Deployment' -> Deployment (context is energy transition)
        'Participation Willingness' -> Participation or Willingness (choose based on emphasis)

        GENERALIZATION HIERARCHY (Apply from most general that preserves meaning):
        Level 1 - Core concept: Acceptance, Deployment, Policy, Trust, Cost
        Level 2 - Qualified concept (when distinction matters): Public_Acceptance, Community_Trust
        Level 3 - Full specification (only when Level 2 loses critical meaning): Offshore_Wind_Acceptance

        DECISION RULE FOR GRANULARITY:
        Choose the most specific label that remains clearly supported by the excerpt
        and reusable across documents.
        Do NOT guess corpus frequency during extraction.

        PRESERVE SPECIFICITY ONLY WHEN:
        Stakeholder experience meaningfully differs (Procedural_Justice is not Justice)
        Technical precision required (Life_Cycle_Assessment vs generic Assessment)
        Core theoretical distinction (Acceptance is not Resistance, Policy is not Regulation)
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

# ============================================
# CAMPOS DE ONTOLOGY
# ============================================

ONTOLOGY FIELDS
    REQUIRED ontology_description
    OPTIONAL topic, aspect, dimension, confidence, reasoning, rgt_element_a, rgt_element_b, theoretical_significance
END ONTOLOGY FIELDS

FIELD ontology_description TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Semantic definition of the concept in the domain of social acceptance of energy technologies.
    GUIDELINES
        Provide a semantic definition of the concept (40-80 words).
        Base the definition primarily on usage contexts where the concept appears
        in annotations (excerpts and chains).

        Structure the definition with these four components:
        1. Core meaning: What the concept represents in the energy transition domain.
        2. Conceptual nature: Essential characteristics distinguishing it from related concepts.
        3. Stakeholder understanding: How it is perceived or experienced in practice.
        4. Domain context: Its established meaning in energy transition acceptance literature.

        Focus on WHAT the factor IS, not on its analytical classification.
        Use directional connections and co-factors as secondary clarification only.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD topic TYPE TOPIC
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Topic or thematic domain of the concept (dynamic higher-level hierarchical category)
    GUIDELINES
        Assign a broad, parent-level thematic category.
        Target: 12-18 conceptually distinct parent categories across the full corpus.

        Suggested parent categories (illustrative, non-exhaustive):
        Governance, Economics, Worldview, Technology, Environment, Social,
        Knowledge, Behavior, Communication, Infrastructure, Planning,
        Research, Participation, Risk, Actors.

        Always choose parent-level categories, NOT subtypes.

        Self-check before assigning:
        1. Does it fit an existing parent category? Use it.
        2. Is the category at the same granularity level as others? Must be parent-level.
        3. Would 3+ other factors fit in this category? If not, it is too specific.
        4. If the factor is part of a system, classify by the holonym (whole), not the part.
        5. If the factor is a specific subtype, classify by the hypernym (parent), not the subtype.

        GOOD: 'Regulatory Framework' -> Governance | 'Investment Cost' -> Economics
        BAD: 'Policy & Regulation' (too specific) -> Use Governance
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD aspect TYPE ORDERED
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Herman Dooyeweerd's Dutch reformational philosopher modal aspects (order is significant)
    VALUES
        [0] Undefined: Not available
        [1] Quantitative: Measurements, statistics, discrete quantities
        [2] Spatial: Location, geography, spatial relationships
        [3] Kinematic: Movement, flow, temporal dynamics
        [4] Physical: Material properties, forces, energy systems
        [5] Biotic: Ecological, environmental, impacts on health and life
        [6] Sensitive: Perception, consciousness, emotional responses
        [7] Analytical: Research, analysis, methodological elements
        [8] Formative: Planning, design, innovation, cultural context
        [9] Lingual: Communication, documentation, information, language
        [10] Social: Community relations, networks, collective dynamics
        [11] Economic: Costs, markets, finance, economic incentives
        [12] Aesthetic: Visual qualities, aesthetics, harmony, beauty
        [13] Juridical: Legal, regulatory, governance frameworks
        [14] Ethical: Moral responsibility, equity, duty, justice
        [15] Fiducial: Trust, belief, values, worldview, faith
    END VALUES
    GUIDELINES
        Classify the factor's primary modal nature using the Dooyeweerd framework.
        The semantic domain of the factor name determines the base classification.

        PRIMARY classification mapping:
        Economic terms -> aspect 11
        Governance/regulation -> aspect 13
        Psychological/trust/attitudes -> aspect 15 or 6
        Environmental/impact/ecology -> aspect 5
        Technical/method/assessment -> aspect 7
        Social/community/participation -> aspect 10

        CLASSIFICATION PRIORITY HIERARCHY:
        1. PRIMARY: Factor semantic meaning and usage context.
           The semantic domain of the factor name determines the base classification.
           Inspect all usage context examples to refine meaning.
        2. SECONDARY: Directional profile (refinement in ambiguous cases only).
           Chain direction does NOT determine the aspect.
           Factors may operate as drivers, prerequisites, barriers, or outcomes.
           Recurrent barrier role -> may indicate barrier-like interpretation.
           Recurrent enabling role -> may indicate facilitator-like interpretation.
           Recurrent effects on perception/behavior -> may suggest perceptual or trust pathways.
        3. TERTIARY: Co-occurrence patterns (clarification only).
        4. QUATERNARY: Statistical profile (evidence strength refinement).
        5. QUINTERNARY: Hierarchical and partitive semantics (taxonomic logic).

        Critical rule: Semantic meaning > directional profile > co-occurrence > statistics.

        TAXONOMIC LOGIC:
        Hypernym-Hyponym: If the factor is a specific subtype, identify its hypernym
        and classify at the parent-level category.
        Meronym-Holonym: If the factor is part of a larger system, orient
        classification by the holonym, not the isolated part.
        Function: Prevent over-fragmentation, maintain stable parent-level categories,
        ensure consistent semantic grouping, align with formal ontologies.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD dimension TYPE ORDERED
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Dimensions of the social acceptance of energy technologies (Developed by Rolf Wüstenhagen - Maarten Wolsink - Mary Jean Bürer)
    VALUES
        [0] Undefined: Not classified or not applicable
        [1] Community_Acceptance: Residents, local actors and community stakeholders
        [2] Market_Acceptance: Consumers, investors, market competitiveness
        [3] Socio-Political_Acceptance: Institutions, society, governance frameworks
        [4] Technical-Scientific: Scientific, analytical and technical evaluation        
    END VALUES
    GUIDELINES
        Assign the Wuestenhagen social acceptance dimension based on stakeholder relevance.

        Use co-occurrence patterns to disambiguate:
        Co-occurs with policy/governance -> dimension 3 (Socio-Political)
        Co-occurs with community/trust/local -> dimension 1 (Community)
        Co-occurs with market/finance/consumer -> dimension 2 (Market)
        Co-occurs with research/method/analysis -> dimension 4 (Technical-Scientific)

        If no clear dimension can be assigned, use 0 (Undefined).
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD confidence TYPE ENUMERATED
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Confidence level in concept classification based on frequency and sources.
    VALUES
        LOW: Low frequency or limited sources (emerging or specialized concept)
        MEDIUM: Moderate frequency with reasonable support from multiple sources
        HIGH: High frequency and broad empirical support from various sources        
    END VALUES
    GUIDELINES
        Assign confidence based on statistical evidence:
        HIGH: frequency > 10 AND sources > 5 (central, well-supported concept)
        MEDIUM: frequency 5-10 OR sources 3-5 (moderately supported)
        LOW: frequency < 5 OR sources < 3 (emerging or specialized concept)

        High frequency with broad sources -> prioritize stable parent categories,
        boost confidence to HIGH.
        Low frequency or narrow sources -> flag as tentative, assign LOW or MEDIUM.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD reasoning TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Analytical justification for the classification of the concept (aspect, dimension, confidence)
    GUIDELINES
        Justify classification decisions in 40-60 words with these 5 components:

        1. Aspect classification (10-15 words): State aspect number and explain why
           the factor belongs to this modal aspect.
        2. Functional role from chains (10-15 words): Identify whether the factor
           mainly appears as driver, prerequisite, barrier, mediator, or outcome.
        3. Relational patterns (10-15 words): Note most frequent co-factors that clarify
           the operational context.
        4. Dimension assignment (10-15 words): State the Wuestenhagen dimension and
           justify based on stakeholder relevance.
        5. Evidence strength (10-15 words): Evaluate frequency and sources for robustness.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD rgt_element_a TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Pole A of the bipolar construct (Repertory Grid Theory) - positive/functional pole
    GUIDELINES
        Create the POSITIVE/FUNCTIONAL pole of a Repertory Grid Theory bipolar construct.
        Transform the unidimensional factor into a contrastive pair that allows
        comparative evaluation.

        The positive pole expresses the functional, desirable, or maximized form.

        Construction GUIDELINES by factor domain:
        Psychological/Social: Use natural oppositions reflecting stakeholder experience.
          Trust -> "High Trust" | Acceptance -> "High Acceptance"
        Economic: Reverse polarity for cost-related concepts.
          Cost -> "Low Cost" (low is desirable) | Benefit -> "High Benefit"
        Governance/Institutional: Use functional poles.
          Governance -> "Effective Governance" | Policy -> "Clear Policy Framework"
        Environmental: Use positive impact directionality.
          Impact -> "Positive Impact" | Environment -> "Environmental Protection"
        Abstract: Convert to functional polarities.
          Energy Independence -> "Energy Independence"
          Community Engagement -> "High Community Engagement"

        Critical: Poles must be empirically distinguishable, evaluatively meaningful,
        and preserve the original concept's semantic core.
        Use clear, stakeholder-comprehensible language.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD rgt_element_b TYPE TEXT
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    DESCRIPTION Pole B of the bipolar construct (Repertory Grid Theory) - negative/reduced pole
    GUIDELINES
        Create the NEGATIVE/REDUCED pole of a Repertory Grid Theory bipolar construct.
        This pole expresses the absent, reduced, compromised, or opposite form
        of the factor.

        Construction GUIDELINES by factor domain:
        Psychological/Social: Use natural oppositions reflecting stakeholder experience.
          Trust -> "Low Trust" | Acceptance -> "Low Acceptance"
        Economic: Reverse polarity for cost-related concepts.
          Cost -> "High Cost" (high is undesirable) | Benefit -> "Low Benefit"
        Governance/Institutional: Use dysfunctional poles.
          Governance -> "Ineffective Governance" | Policy -> "Unclear Policy Framework"
        Environmental: Use negative impact directionality.
          Impact -> "Negative Impact" | Environment -> "Environmental Degradation"
        Abstract: Convert to reduced/absent polarities.
          Energy Independence -> "Energy Dependence"
          Community Engagement -> "Low Community Engagement"

        Critical: Poles must be empirically distinguishable, evaluatively meaningful,
        and preserve the original concept's semantic core.
        Avoid purely semantic oppositions; focus on functional/experiential contrasts.
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD

FIELD theoretical_significance TYPE SCALE
    SCOPE ONTOLOGY
    FORMAT [0..5]
    DESCRIPTION Theoretical significance of the concept (0=low, 5=high)
    GUIDELINES
        Rate the theoretical significance of the concept on a 0-5 scale.
        Base the rating on frequency, source diversity, and centrality in causal chains.

        0: No theoretical significance identified
        1: Peripheral concept, rarely appears in chains
        2: Minor concept, appears in few chains with limited centrality
        3: Moderate significance, appears in multiple chains across several sources
        4: High significance, central node in many chains, well-supported empirically
        5: Core concept, foundational to the theoretical framework, appears across
           many sources with high frequency and diverse relational patterns
    END GUIDELINES
END FIELD
